---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PlatformLinks from '../components/PlatformLinks.astro';
import { SITE, AUTHOR } from '../site.config';
import { breadcrumbSchema, PERSON_ID, ORG_ID } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'About Generative Futures';
const description =
  'Generative Futures is a project on the political economy of AI, escaping techno-determinism and articulating a positive vision for what comes after. By Phil Bell.';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: title,
    description,
    url: `${SITE.url}/about`,
    mainEntity: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    about: { '@id': PERSON_ID },
  },
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'About', path: '/about' },
  ]),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/about" schema={schema}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>What are Generative Futures?</h1>

      <blockquote>
        “The myth of technological and political and social inevitability is a
        powerful tranquilizer of the conscience… But, in fact, there are actors!”
        <br /><span class="muted">Joseph Weizenbaum</span>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="lede" style="color:var(--ink);">
        Technologies are malleable. The way they are used, and their impact, is
        not inevitable, it is the product of a negotiation between the people who
        use them, institutions, and the affordances of the technology itself.
      </p>

      <h2>Escaping techno-determinism</h2>
      <p>
        We chose the name <strong>Generative Futures</strong> because the debate
        about AI is currently trapped between two poles: <em>inevitability</em>
        (the belief that the effects of AI are unavoidable) and <em>nostalgia</em>
        (the desire to simply reject it). Both are dead ends.
      </p>
      <p>
        The history of AI has been highly contingent. If it weren’t for GPUs,
        cheap capital from quantitative easing, cheap energy from fracking, and
        rising scepticism about social media, AI could have taken a different
        path. Many researchers believe the dominant approach, transformer-based
        language models, isn’t optimal; it simply won the “hardware lottery.”
        Diving into the details reveals how contingent our technological present
        is, and how malleable the future could be.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whether technologies replace people or empower them is, as economists
        like Daron Acemoglu and David Autor argue, a political choice, not a
        technical destiny. “AI will not decide how AI is used,” as Autor puts it.
        “Its constructive and destructive applications are boundless.”
      </p>

      <h2>Understanding AI in order to shape it</h2>
      <p>
        The barrier to entry on the AI debate is currently too high, much as
        economics became forbiddingly technical before 2008, sidelining the
        political questions until a crisis forced them open. We think the
        technical AI conversation is similarly too narrow. AI is political, and
        keeping its technicalities reserved for a small group in the Bay Area is
        good for no one. Generative Futures speaks to people building AI and AI
        safety, and writes explainers that make those ideas legible to a wider
        set of actors.
      </p>

      <h2>A positive vision</h2>
      <p>
        Almost no one has articulated a positive vision for what comes after
        powerful AI. Without one, we risk defaulting to whatever emerges from
        market and geopolitical dynamics. As Milton Friedman observed, when a
        crisis comes, “the actions that are taken depend on the ideas that are
        lying around.” Because we don’t know when an opportunity or crisis will
        arrive, we should start generating those ideas now.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Get involved</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Subscribe</strong> to receive new posts.</li>
          <li><strong>Join the reading group</strong> at Newspeak House in London.</li>
          <li><strong>Write with us</strong>, we’re looking for contributors.</li>
          <li><strong>Collaborate</strong>, suggest a podcast guest or co-host an episode.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="hero__actions" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://genfutures.substack.com" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Subscribe on Substack</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>About the author</h2>
      <p>{AUTHOR.bio}</p>

      <hr class="divider" />
      <h2>Find Generative Futures everywhere</h2>
      <PlatformLinks />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
